WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.600
Good to have you on fascinating frontiers, episode 87 for June 1st, 2026.

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:09.280
Starting the week with the latest from Space and Science.

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:15.000
A rare parent's stability supernova may have erased one of the universe's most massive

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:17.580
stars without leaving any remnant behind.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:24.140
The explosion appears to have completely destroyed its pergenitor star, leaving no neutron star

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:25.580
or black hole remnant.

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:34.900
Observations match models of stars between 140 and 260 solar masses that undergo runaway oxygen fusion.

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:41.980
The event was posted to ArcSive on May 15th and stands as one of the clearest examples recorded.

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:48.260
Data include light curve shapes and spectral features that rule out other supernova classes.

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:53.180
Additional archival searches are planned to locate similar past events.

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:59.900
Now, on a completely different note, the identification of a candidate pair instability supernova

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:06.720
stands out because the star is thought to have vanished entirely rather than collapsing into a compact remnant.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:14.480
Light curve and spectral data align with theoretical predictions for stars in the 140 to 260 solar

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:17.960
mass range, undergoing explosive oxygen burning.

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:23.660
This event supplies one of the strongest observational anchors yet for models that previously

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:25.460
rested on simulations alone.

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:30.020
Arcival searches are now underway to locate additional examples.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:35.480
What fraction of the first stars ended their lives this way remains an open question.

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:38.640
You know what is fascinating about this?

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:44.140
If the sun were scaled to the size of a basketball, a pair instability supernova

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:49.280
progenitor would be a star roughly the diameter of Earth's orbit around that basketball.

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:56.980
Inside such a star, temperatures climb past 300 million degrees and photons spontaneously convert

00:01:56.980 --> 00:02:01.580
into electron positron pairs, robbing the core of pressure support.

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:07.900
The resulting collapse triggers runaway oxygen fusion that releases energy equivalent to detonating

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:15.040
10 to the power of 52 irks in seconds. No compact remnant survives because the entire star is

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:21.600
unbound. The lingering mystery is why so few clear examples appear in current surveys despite

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:28.500
the expected abundance of massive stars in the early universe. The May 28th test flight failure

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:35.120
removes a key cargo vehicle from near-term lunar logistics planning. NASA had intended to use

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:41.120
new Glenn for delivering infrastructure ahead of crude landings. The company now faces an estimated

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:48.200
year or longer before the next launch attempt. This setback compounds existing delays in the overall

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:54.100
Artemis timeline. The grounded booster removes one of the few vehicles capable of lifting large

00:02:54.100 --> 00:03:00.800
national security payloads, remaining operators face increased demand and longer wait times for rides.

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:07.380
The reusable booster lifted four chin fans satellites into orbit without prior public notice.

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:12.900
The launch marked the first flight of the 12B variant designed for rapid turnaround.

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:18.400
Payload deployment occurred successfully into the target low-Earth orbit.

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:24.420
Industry analysts will watch the booster's recovery attempt for signs of reusability progress.

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:30.900
Four experimental satellites reached orbit on a hypergolic rocket to validate phone-to-satellite

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:36.540
connectivity. The mission supports multiple national projects aimed at extending coverage to

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:42.300
remote regions. Early tests will measure signal strength and latency from handheld devices.

00:03:43.060 --> 00:03:48.020
The new catalog reveals mergers that occurred in environments previously missed by earlier

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:53.840
gravitational wave detectors. Data come from improved waveform modeling that separates

00:03:53.840 --> 00:04:00.680
overlapping signals in LIGO, Virgo, CAGRA archives. The findings tighten constraints on how massive

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:07.540
stars evolve in dense clusters. The newly confirmed worlds orbit their stars at distances that place

00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:13.620
them among the widest known transiting systems. Their youth allows direct comparison with formation

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:20.460
models that predict rapid migration inward. Transit timing and radial velocity follow-up provided

00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:26.740
the orbital periods and masses. The three planets reach peak visibility in the western sky after

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:33.000
sunset throughout the month. Venus and Jupiter appear close together, while Mercury climbs higher

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:39.200
each evening. Observers can track the changing separations with binoculars or the naked eye from

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:47.780
mid-northern latitudes. James Lyck's $700,000 bequest funded the 36-inch refractor that became the

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:54.340
world's largest telescope at the time. The trust moved to the University of California on June 1st

00:04:54.340 --> 00:05:00.460
after years of construction on Mount Hamilton. The site choice exploited high elevation air clarity

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:06.640
for sharper stellar images. The second full moon of May appeared smaller than average because it

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:12.580
occurred near apogee. Photographers recorded the event from multiple continents under varying weather

00:05:12.580 --> 00:05:20.040
conditions. This was the smallest full moon of 2026. The rare stellar system provides a natural

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:26.220
laboratory for studying the extreme physics behind repeating radio signals. University of Sydney

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:32.820
astronomers combined radio and optical data to pinpoint the source environment. Further monitoring

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:39.180
will track how the system's orbital motion modulates the signal properties. The new bonds allow

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:44.980
spaceports to issue debt for infrastructure projects that previously relied on limited public funding.

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:50.900
This change targets facilities that support orbital launches and reentry operations.

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:57.620
Observers expect the first issuances to target upgrades at existing coastal and inland sites.

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:03.680
The guide lists exact times and dates when earth lies directly between the sun and moon.

00:06:04.220 --> 00:06:10.920
It also notes which full moon's coincide with perigee or apogee. The composite merges at 1978

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:17.920
IBM punch card simulation, modern numerical runs, a photograph of the M87 black hole,

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:25.000
and an artist's drawing. Blue spots mark the earliest computed photon rings from the 1978 model.

